 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
-*   **Precision:** Rendering error computation must be stable to avoid oscillating subdivision patterns. Not given in the sources.
-*   **Boundaries:** Quadtree nodes must align with pixel boundaries to ensure correct ray mapping. Not given in the sources.
-*   **Degenerate Input:** If an image is uniform, the quadtree will remain at the root level, casting minimal rays. If an image is highly noisy, the quadtree may reach max depth everywhere, resulting in uniform sampling. Not given in the sources.
+*   **Precision:** Rendering error computation must be stable to avoid oscillating subdivision patterns [G].
+*   **Boundaries:** Quadtree nodes must align with pixel boundaries to ensure correct ray mapping [G].
+*   **Degenerate Input:** If an image is uniform, the quadtree will remain at the root level, casting minimal rays [G]. If an image is highly noisy, the quadtree may reach max depth everywhere, resulting in uniform sampling [G].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** The method reduces the total number of rays $M$ significantly compared to uniform sampling, thereby reducing the time spent in volume rendering per epoch [S1]. The overhead of quadtree management and error computation adds a small constant cost [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the quadtree structure for each image. The size is proportional to the number of nodes, which is bounded by $4 \times \text{Image Resolution}$ in the worst case (full subdivision) [G].
